  "image_name": "04bb47e06ba93fe130a54a033a613f32.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes four common ways soft, flexible structures can be actuated. In all cases, a simple strip with an asymmetric or patterned top surface is driven to bend or deform by different physical inputs.\n\nOne mechanism uses fluidic actuation: a channel connected to an inlet/outlet bends when pressure is applied. Positive pressure inflates one side and produces one curvature, while negative pressure or suction bends it in the opposite direction. A second method uses a dielectric elastomer: a layered elastomer with compliant electrodes is shown first at rest, then under an applied voltage. The voltage causes the film to contract through its thickness and expand in-plane, indicating electrically driven shape change.\n\nA third approach is electrothermal actuation. A coiled or serpentine element changes shape between a cooler state and a hotter state, implying that electrical heating alters its dimensions and drives motion. The last method is tendon-driven deformation, where a tendon runs along one side of the soft body; pulling the tendon creates tension and bends the structure inward.\n\nOverall, the figure compares pressure-, voltage-, heat-, and tendon-based strategies for producing controlled deformation in soft actuators.",
